From 6fb29b543a45fb5d1310e43ec729bafcfdb0afc9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 12:02:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(android): real present-owner weave session + batched
 AHardwareBuffer submit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

M1 turns the M2 seam skeleton into a working weave client. It is the mac client
one-for-one, on a different substrate:

  * dlopen libopenxr_loader.so + libvulkan.so by SONAME. Nothing is linked and
    nothing is bundled, because ipc_client_check_git_tag() strncmps the runtime
    CLIENT library's compiled-in u_git_tag against the running service's — so
    the client .so must be the one the Khronos loader pulls out of the INSTALLED
    runtime package. The Android analogue of patch 0001 vendors the LOADER only.
  * xrInitializeLoaderKHR(VM, Context) then xrCreateInstance with
    XR_KHR_android_create_instance + XR_KHR_vulkan_enable2 + XR_DXR_weave, plus
    display_info and view_rig when advertised (rig rejection retries weave-only
    rather than killing inline-3D, as on mac).
  * A Vulkan-bound present-owner session: no xrBeginSession, no xrWaitFrame, no
    swapchain. The VkDevice exists ONLY to satisfy xrCreateSession — no weave
    pixel moves through it. Input and output are AHardwareBuffers crossing to
    the runtime service, and the browser-side copy is Skia in Viz.
  * xrWeaveBindWindow2DXR with a chained XrWeaveWindowGeometryDXR. REQUIRED on
    Android: no window handle to read a position off, and a pure move raises no
    resize (ADR-036 D6). SetWindowGeometryAndroid() is the seam M3's
    Choreographer feed replaces; the GPU provider publishes the fullscreen
    placeholder until then.
  * Batched submit: XrWeaveSubmitInfoDXR -> XrWeaveSubmitRectsDXR (<=32) ->
    XrWeaveSubmitHandlesDXR with inputKind =
    XR_WEAVE_HANDLE_KIND_AHARDWAREBUFFER_DXR. Declaring the kind is mandatory —
    PLATFORM_DEFAULT would have the service dereference a non-pointer.
    firstChunk on the frame's first chunk (v5 transparent-gap clear).

Spec v7 is a hard floor: the handle kind, xrWeaveBindWindow2DXR and the geometry
chain are all load-bearing, so a pre-v7 runtime cannot serve this path at all
and we say so once instead of failing per frame.

Everything stays dormant without DXR_IPC_FD — a device with no runtime loads no
loader, creates no instance, and reports inline-3D unsupported.

Refs #1056, docs/android-port.md M1

Two device-loop fixes are folded in here, because they are what makes this
commit's xrInitializeLoaderKHR call actually succeed:

  * A REAL application Context. The GPU process first reached
    xrInitializeLoaderKHR with a null applicationContext, so the Khronos loader
    bailed out of LoaderInitData::initialize() and every later xr* call failed
    ("AddManifestFilesAndroid unable to add manifest files / LoaderInitData not
    initialized", then "RuntimeInterface::LoadRuntime cannot run because
    xrInitializeLoaderKHR was not successfully called"). The cause was
    env->FindClass(): InitializeCoreAndroid() runs on a natively created
    Chromium thread with no Java frames on its stack, so JNI FindClass resolves
    against the BOOTSTRAP class loader and cannot see ANY application class. It
    returned null, the exception was cleared, and the loader got a null Context.
    The failure was invisible because the XrResult was discarded — it is now
    checked, and the path bails loudly instead of proceeding into a
    guaranteed-failing xrEnumerateInstanceExtensionProperties.

  * GENERATED JNI, not a name lookup. base::android::GetClass() fixes the
    class-loader problem but still resolves org.chromium.base.ContextUtils by
    SOURCE NAME, and chrome_public_apk is a release (is_debug=false) build: R8
    shrinks and obfuscates our Java, so that name does not exist at run time
    ("org.chromium.base.ContextUtils not resolvable" / "no JavaVM/Context for
    xrInitializeLoaderKHR ... inline-3D disabled"). DisplayXrContextBridge is a
    @CalledByNative shim returning ContextUtils.getApplicationContextUnsafe();
    jni_zero emits the matching -keep rules, so the reference survives
    shrinking — the R8 mapping shows the class and its method mapping to
    THEMSELVES, while a plain field in the sibling connector class maps
    RUNTIME_PACKAGES -> a. That contrast is the whole point: generated JNI is
    the only R8-proof way for native to name our own Java.
    getApplicationContextUnsafe() deliberately — a null Context is something
    the caller reports and disables inline-3D over, not a CHECK failure.

    The remaining reflection in DisplayXrRuntimeConnector.connectTo() is safe
    and audited: its targets (org.freedesktop.monado.ipc.Client, its
    blockingConnect method, its fd field) all live in the INSTALLED runtime
    apk, and R8 shrinks only ours.

